# netsurf (MacSurf bridge): a working CSSOM, a real DOMException, rendered innerText
# Generated 2026-09-30
#
# Google's "second wall" (hardware run 27: jserror at ysa {{0}}:313:407,
# sim r4: $ra {{0}}:307:407). Every module google loads calls
# _F_installCss(css): it appends a <style> with the module's CSS, which
# ends in a `sentinel{}` rule, and checks the CSSOM saw it -- the sheet in
# document.styleSheets whose ownerNode is that <style> must have cssRules,
# the last a CSSStyleRule with selectorText "sentinel". The bridge's
# document.styleSheets built fresh objects with cssRules always [] and
# insertRule a no-op, so the check reported error "oc" ("zc" in the device
# build) for every module. The report is non-fatal (_.$e), but the same
# stub meant CSS-in-JS (emotion, styled-components, JSS: rules added with
# insertRule) got no styling at all.
#
# vita_polyfill.js:
# - CSSOM: one CSSStyleSheet per <style>/<link rel=stylesheet>, kept on
#   the element (document.styleSheets[i] === el.sheet); StyleSheet,
#   CSSStyleSheet, CSSRule + CSSStyleRule, CSSMediaRule, CSSSupportsRule,
#   CSSImportRule, CSSFontFaceRule, CSSPageRule, CSSKeyframesRule/
#   CSSKeyframeRule, CSSNamespaceRule, CSSLayer*Rule, CSSContainerRule,
#   CSSGroupingRule, CSSConditionRule, with the CSSRule type constants.
#   cssRules is parsed on demand from the <style>'s text by a brace/
#   string/comment-aware scan (regex-driven: nested group rules,
#   keyframes, at-statements) and re-parsed when the text changes; <link>
#   sheets keep an empty list (the engine holds their text).
#   insertRule/deleteRule (sheet and group rules; IndexSizeError,
#   SyntaxError), addRule/removeRule, appendRule/findRule, rule.style
#   (getPropertyValue/setProperty/removeProperty/cssText; camelCase where
#   Proxy exists). Changes are written back as the <style>'s text once
#   per microtask -- the engine restyles that like any script edit of a
#   <style> (patch 169 makes that restyle happen after load) -- so a
#   burst of inserts is one restyle. selectorText "a, b" and keyText
#   "0%"/"100%" read as browsers serialize them. No replace()/
#   replaceSync(): Lit and others would then use constructable sheets +
#   adoptedStyleSheets, which the engine does not render.
# - DOMException: the bridge's is an empty stub (macsurf_qjs.c's list of
#   constructors), so every `new DOMException(msg, 'AbortError')` in this
#   file had no name, message or code. Replaced only when the one there
#   loses its name; codes and the *_ERR constants.
# - innerText / outerText were textContent: <style>/<script> text, hidden
#   and display:none subtrees came along and nothing marked a line.
#   google.com keeps its <style> blocks in <body>: its body.innerText was
#   30 KB of CSS, and the scorecard (which reads visible text this way)
#   saw none of the page's words. Now the rendered text: script, style,
#   template, noscript, [hidden] and box-less (display:none) subtrees are
#   left out -- the bridge's __gcsNative answers a display only for an
#   element with a box --, block boxes start and end lines, <br> breaks
#   one, <p> is set off by a blank line, table cells by tabs, white space
#   collapses outside <pre>/<textarea>.
# Tests: JS-cssom.html 8/8 (with patch 169), JS-innertext.html 8/8; both
# pages also 8/8 in Chromium, and the innerText implementation itself was
# run in Chromium against the same cases.
